Stale lifecycle metadata can delete recreated-bucket objects
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/2078ef40ad488191ad79d7f7d5effa6d?sev=
Criticality: high (attack path: high)
Status: fixed
Fixed by: 3778d0e2cd2458434c25d1db91346cf36a7eea4b
Fix link: https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/3778d0e2cd2458434c25d1db91346cf36a7eea4b

# Fix Details
The fix adds a durable bucket-incarnation fence to lifecycle sweep mutations instead
of relying on removed process-local bucket-PG serialization.

- Server-core lifecycle sweeps pass the claimed `bucket_incarnation_generation`
  into every per-candidate lifecycle mutation, including current object expiry,
  noncurrent version expiry, expired delete-marker cleanup, abort-incomplete MPU,
  and finishing already-Aborting MPU cleanup.
- Storage lifecycle context loading now takes the expected bucket incarnation and
  returns no work if the reloaded bucket row belongs to a newer same-name bucket.
- Lifecycle bucket-write proof acquisition now verifies the acquired proof matches
  the expected incarnation. A proof for a recreated bucket is released and the
  stale lifecycle mutation stops.
- Matching pending lifecycle commands are only converged for the current sweep
  when the command-owned bucket-write proof matches the expected incarnation.
- Regressions cover both race windows: bucket delete/finalize/recreate before
  lifecycle context reload, with lifecycle enabled again on the recreated bucket,
  and bucket delete/finalize/recreate after context load but before bucket-write
  proof acquisition. The new bucket-scoped hook tests are serialized to avoid
  parallel test hook interference.

