# Summary
Introduced: the commit removed the process-local bucket-PG guard from load_bucket_lifecycle_context and related bucket request paths without adding an equivalent durable incarnation fence. Object lifecycle mutations still acquire bucket-write reservations, but those reservations are for the current active bucket and are not checked against the bucket metadata/lifecycle incarnation that was originally read.
The commit removes the bucket metadata primary mutex guard from lifecycle context loading and bucket creation/control paths. Lifecycle mutation helpers now load bucket_info and raw_lifecycle, drop any bucket-incarnation serialization, and later acquire a durable bucket-write proof for whatever active bucket currently exists. The reservation acquisition is not passed the bucket_incarnation_generation that was read with the lifecycle configuration. If a bucket is deleted and then recreated with the same name between these steps, stale lifecycle rules, versioning state, and owner data from the old incarnation can be applied to objects in the new incarnation. For non-versioned buckets the expected VersionId is VersionId::Null, so a stale lifecycle candidate can match and delete a fresh object with the same key in the recreated bucket. For versioned/suspended buckets, the old versioning state and owner identity can also drive the wrong delete-marker vs hard-delete behavior. This can allow an authenticated old bucket owner who can configure lifecycle/delete/recreate timing, or a racing lifecycle worker after bucket-name reuse, to corrupt or delete data belonging to the new bucket incarnation.

