## Narrative
The finding is real and in-scope. The commit removed the bucket metadata primary guard from BucketLifecycleContext and from bucket creation paths. Lifecycle expiry now reads bucket_info/raw_lifecycle, later locks only the object metadata PG, and then acquires a durable bucket-write reservation for whatever active bucket currently exists. The reservation record stores the current active bucket incarnation, and validation checks it against the current bucket row, but no caller-supplied expected lifecycle-context incarnation is checked. The earlier validation PoC used the normal storage lifecycle path with a test pause to demonstrate that after deleting and recreating a bucket with the same name, a fresh non-versioned object with the same key was deleted by stale lifecycle state. Exploitation requires authenticated bucket-owner/admin privileges and a tight race, so likelihood is low, but the data integrity/availability impact can be high when bucket names are reused across ownership boundaries.
## Controls
- AWS SigV4 authentication in auth/server-http before S3 operation dispatch
- Bucket owner/account-admin authorization for DeleteBucket and PutLifecycleConfiguration
- Bucket policy authorization checks for bucket control actions
- Lifecycle sweep claims include bucket incarnation but are not propagated as a mutation fence
- Durable bucket-write reservations exist but currently reserve the current active bucket without comparing to the lifecycle context's read incarnation
- HTTP max connection and max in-flight request limits
- Optional TLS via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
- Default listen address is localhost unless ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR is changed
## Blindspots
- This stage used static repository evidence plus the earlier validation report; no additional HTTP-level end-to-end exploit was run.
- No Kubernetes, Terraform, Docker Compose, or load balancer manifests were found in the inspected repository depth, so public exposure is inferred only from application configuration.
- The executable PoC verified bucket-incarnation reuse deletion, but a different-account recreated-bucket variant was not dynamically proven here.
- Actual exploitability depends on deployment concurrency, lifecycle sweep timing, bucket deletion finalization behavior, and whether the service is operated as single-tenant or multi-tenant.
